some time after the rest of it had gone  well i've often seen a cat without a  grin  thought alice but a grin without a cat  it's the most curious thing i ever saw  in all my life  this grin might be referring to abstract  or  pure mathematics that it doesn't  necessarily have a cat to go along with  it  which would be the real life  applications or even  obvious uses of what the research  mathematicians are working on  they're doing it just for the joy of the  mathematics itself  and are living in the abstract world of  the equations  further into wonderland alice is talking  to griffin  and mock turtle she asked them about  lessons  and how many hours a day did you do  lessons said alice  in a hurry to change the subject ten  hours the first day  he said the mock turtle nine the next  and so on  what a curious plan exclaimed alice  that's the reason they're called lessons  because they listen  from day to day this was quite a new  idea to alice  and she thought it over a little before  she made her next remark  then the 11th day must have been a  holiday of course it was  said the mock turtle and how did you  manage on the 12th  alice went on eagerly that's enough  about lessons  this might be a fun way to introduce the  idea of negative numbers  something that also puzzled early  mathematicians  in this case what would happen on the  12th day would the students start to  teach the teacher  then we go to the famous tea party scene  there's the mad hatter  a hair and a mouse and alice is also  informed of a fourth character  time who is absent from the party due to  a falling out with the hatter  as a result the three at the tea party  are forever  stuck at tea time they're forever going  around and around the table  the hair takes out a watch and alice  remarks  what a funny watch it tells the day of  the month  and doesn't tell what o'clock it is why  should it  muttered the hatter does your watch tell  you what year it is  of course not alice replied very readily  but that's because it stays the same  year for such a long time  which is just the case with mine said  the hatter  later he says that it's always six  o'clock  is that the reason so many tea things  are put out here alice asked  yes that's it said the hatter it's  always tea time  and we've no time to wash the things  between wiles  then you keep moving around i suppose  said alice  exactly so said the hatter now it might  be nothing  but the idea of three characters start  going around and around  and not making much sense without time  shares a few similarities  with the idea of quaternions something  that was introduced  not too long before the story was  written quaternions consist of  three spatial dimensions i j and k  which are imaginary and a fourth  dimension which is  real and that is time perhaps at the tea  party  the three characters are the imaginary  units and without time  they don't really make sense a property  of quaternions  is that when you multiply them they are  non-commutative  which means the order actually matters  normal multiplication  such as 3 times 4 is the same as  4 times 3 it is commutative but with  quaternions  i times j is not the same as j times i  and there's a scene at the tea party  where this comes up in the language  they are discussing a riddle do you mean  that you think you can find out the  answer to it  said the march here exactly so said  alice  then you should say what you mean the  march here went on  i do alice hastily replied at least  at least i mean what i say that's the  same thing you know  not the same thing a bit said the hatter  you might just as well say  that i see what i eat is the same thing  as  i eat what i see you might just as well  say  added in the march here that i like what  i get  is the same thing as i get what i like  so if the characters at the tea party  are used to a non-commutative form of  multiplication  then that's maybe why they're so  unsatisfied with alice  claiming that the order of words doesn't  matter  something that happens a lot to alice  during the story is that she's often  changing size  and having to eat or drink something to  return  to the right proportions a caterpillar  even tells alice  to keep her temper and alice interprets  this to mean  to not get angry however with our  mathematical eyes in place  we could see that carol is making a play  on words  and that there is a more mathematical  definition of temper  that would have been more familiar to  victorians at the time  and has to do with scale and proportions  we see examples of both enlargements and  stretches  which affect alice's body differently we  tend to use the word enlargement  even if we're making something smaller  we would just use a scale factor  between 0 and 1 in that case and that  mathematical use of the word enlargement  might make sentences like you will grow  shorter  make a little bit more sense stretches  would act about a point  so if alice is stretched about her  middle that would make every point  twice as far away from the middle as it  used to be which might explain why her  neck grows so long  and in this case alice has lost her  mathematical temper  the caterpillar might be telling alice  to keep her body in proportion  no matter what size she is carol did  love  euclidean geometry and wrote some books  based on euclid's elements  and in euclidean geometry it doesn't  matter what the  magnitude of shapes is all that matters  is the ratio between sides or the  proportions  i have seen many other interpretations  of the alice in wonderland story  people claim to have found commentary on  social and political issues or  references  to victorian literature given the  author's background  it does make sense for there to be a  little bit of mathematics in there too  but maybe the nature of the story just  lends itself  to these interpretations regardless  i hope you've enjoyed taking a little  look down the rabbit hole  and let me know if you find any other  mathematical references yourself  if you enjoy playing with math or  finding creative ways to share science  then take a look at this video's sponsor  kiwiko  they create these super cool hands-on  projects and toys  designed to expose people to concepts in  science  technology engineering art and math  they sent me a couple of their monthly  crates i've got the make it yourself  stereo headphones and the  electroluminescent sculptures  i found the projects to contain a good  amount of creativity  and problem solving especially for  people encountering these ideas  for the first time each box comes with  all the supplies needed for that project  instructions and an educational magazine  they cater to different age groups so  you might enjoy the projects for  yourself  or know some kids who you want to share  the joy of making with  and who knows what they might go on to  do with those skills  i made a flamingo something that alice  later uses to play croquet  you can get 50 of your first month of  any crate  by going to kiwico.com tibby's  which will be linked down in the  description below a big thank you to  kiwico  and as always thanks to my patreon  supporters for helping me to make these  videos  a special shout out to today's patreon  cat of the day  xena warrior princess
 

